00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:03.590
in that system.

00:01:03.590 --> 00:01:04.930
Jess.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:07.960
JESSICA FRAZELLE: I'll
introduce Alex Mohr, my coworker

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:10.150
and the technical
team and manager

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:13.990
of some of the Google
Container Engine teams,

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:16.560
including Platform Security.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:19.330
For two and a half years,
he's worked on GKE.

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:24.730
And then before that, he worked
on the Google Compute Engine.

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:26.530
So what we're going
to cover today

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:30.940
as a basic outline is security
boundaries, identity and access

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:34.180
management, runtime
security in a demo,

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:38.550
and then the Kubernetes
roadmap for the future.

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:42.590
So let's just dive right
into the security boundaries.

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:45.320
We have these various
security models.

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:48.130
The way it works today
is a single tenant.

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:52.140
There is one trust boundary,
which is your entire cluster.

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:54.680
And all users are admin.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:58.700
But with some features coming
in Kubernetes 1.6 including

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:02.480
role-based authentication,
which we'll cover more in depth

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:07.550
later, we are moving into
a different model where

00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:09.860
single-tenant
multiple identities--

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:13.210
there's multiple users,
no really enforcement,

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:16.870
but you still treat the entire
cluster as your boundary.

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:19.850
So as long as you
create multiple clusters

00:02:19.850 --> 00:02:22.580
for various projects
or teams that you

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:25.340
don't trust to be on the same
one, then you're totally fine.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:28.070
And of course, spinning
up clusters in GKE

00:02:28.070 --> 00:02:32.090
is super easy, like command line
tools, or you can use the UI.

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:36.380
And then we'll move into a more
cooperative soft multi-tenancy,

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:40.100
where this is more multi-user,
fine-grained authorization

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:41.460
permissions.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.090
It's not entirely
fully hardened,

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:45.950
but it's good enough
for multi-tenancy

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:48.230
inside the same company.

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:51.870
You can control bad actors and
remove them from the cluster.

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:55.760
So what the single
tenant looks like is you

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.480
have your entire
cluster as the boundary.

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:00.934
Team 1 and team 2 you trust
to be on the same cluster,

00:03:00.934 --> 00:03:01.850
so you put them there.

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:05.370
Otherwise, you'd have a new
cluster for whoever that is.

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:07.250
And so you have
their front-end ops

00:03:07.250 --> 00:03:11.240
and their databases running on
the same cluster as each other.

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:13.780
All the nodes have the same
authenticated identity.

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:16.340
All pods have the same
authorization permissions.

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:20.710
And all pods have
network access.

00:03:20.710 --> 00:03:23.840
So now I'll pass it back to Alex
to cover identity and access

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:24.510
management.

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:26.140
ALEX MOHR: Thank you, Jess.

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:29.730
So let's talk about identity
and access management.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:31.740
The first part of this
is authentication--

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:33.390
that is, who are you.

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:35.760
We'll talk in a little
bit about authorization.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:36.990
What can you do?

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:40.500
So user authentication--
who are you?

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:43.630
If you are the little puppet in
the upper right corner there,

00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:46.590
you want to access
a GKE cluster.

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:49.020
One way of doing that
is using bearer tokens,

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:51.360
and another way of doing
that with Container Engine

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:54.790
is that you can use Google
OAuth2 access tokens.

00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:57.030
So for example, if you
run that line on the top

00:03:57.030 --> 00:04:01.060
after you've authenticated with
Google using GCloud Auth Login,

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:03.760
you can go get credentials
for a given cluster.

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:07.470
And that will configure your
cube control config file such

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:09.360
that it will use
those credentials when

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.030
accessing your cluster.

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:15.250
When you then issue your
cube control create command,

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:18.679
passing in some YAML file
that you want to have created,

00:04:18.679 --> 00:04:20.220
then what will happen
is your request

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:22.980
will be sent to the GKE
cluster, the API server that's

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:24.540
hosted for you.

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:26.440
And it will receive
that request.

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:29.130
The Google OAuth2 tokens
will be included in there,

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:32.490
and those will then be
forwarded to GCPs IAM service

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:34.020
to verify them.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:36.390
If that happens,
then, the response

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:39.820
is passed back to the API server
that runs on your cluster,

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:44.470
and you'll then be
authenticated as your identity.

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:47.710
Users are not the only
identity that we care about.

00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:49.420
We also have many
system components.

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:52.660
So a Kubernetes cluster is
not just a single thing.

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:55.660
We have the GKE master,
which has controllers and API

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:57.460
servers and many other bits.

00:04:57.460 --> 00:04:59.380
But your cluster also
consists of the nodes

00:04:59.380 --> 00:05:01.450
that are participating in it.

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:04.240
So the master itself has
a certificate authority,

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:08.202
one CA for the entire cluster.

00:05:08.202 --> 00:05:09.910
And the master is then
given certificates

00:05:09.910 --> 00:05:12.091
that are signed by that CA.

00:05:12.091 --> 00:05:13.840
At the same time, the
nodes in the cluster

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:16.420
are also given node certificates
and keys that are also

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:18.400
signed by that same CA.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:20.990
And when we create these
nodes on your behalf,

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:23.980
we pass along the details of
what those node certificates

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:26.800
are in the VM metadata
server that these nodes are

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:27.940
running on.

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:31.150
And so then the nodes are able
to fetch that key and that cert

00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:32.980
down from the meditative server.

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:34.870
And when they
contact the master,

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:37.339
then you get mutual
authentication.

00:05:37.339 --> 00:05:39.880
So the nodes are going to verify
the master cert when they're

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:42.130
talking to it to make sure
that it's really the master

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:43.970
that they expect it to be.

00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:45.970
And of course, at the
same time then, the master

00:05:45.970 --> 00:05:48.280
can verify that the note is
coming with the cert that

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:50.170
is signed by that
CA, and therefore, it

00:05:50.170 --> 00:05:52.162
should be allowed
to join the cluster.

00:05:52.162 --> 00:05:53.620
And this is important
to stop nodes

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:55.690
that you do not want to
be part of your cluster

00:05:55.690 --> 00:05:56.480
from joining in.

00:05:59.170 --> 00:06:00.970
The third pillar of
authentication we have

00:06:00.970 --> 00:06:02.680
is for workloads.

00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:05.440
So Kubernetes has
support for the things

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:08.440
running inside your cluster
to also have identities.

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:11.560
So maybe you want to have a
cron job-style service that

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:13.091
runs inside your cluster.

00:06:13.091 --> 00:06:15.340
And your cron job is going
to do something every hour,

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:18.220
like contact the
API server to create

00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:20.470
a particular pod or a
deployment, a replica

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:23.557
set, whatever it wants
to do every hour.

00:06:23.557 --> 00:06:25.890
And in that case, the thing
that's running in my cluster

00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:29.740
needs to have some
form of mechanism

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:32.270
for identifying itself
to that API server

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:34.929
so it will have permissions
to do what it wants to do.

00:06:34.929 --> 00:06:36.220
So there are two parts to this.

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:39.435
On the left, you can see we have
Kubernetes service accounts.

00:06:39.435 --> 00:06:40.810
And the way these
are implemented

00:06:40.810 --> 00:06:43.630
is that there is a service
account controller that runs,

00:06:43.630 --> 00:06:46.540
and it signs any jots
for all of the service

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:47.550
accounts in the system.

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:48.940
So if there is a
service account,

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:51.530
a jot will be created for it.

00:06:51.530 --> 00:06:55.376
And those jots then get
stored in the API as secrets.

00:06:55.376 --> 00:06:57.250
And so then when you
want to create something

00:06:57.250 --> 00:06:59.260
that uses a given
service account,

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:01.150
you can specify for
your pod, please

00:07:01.150 --> 00:07:04.030
use specified service account.

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:05.920
And if you don't specify
a service account,

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:08.230
currently the
default is that you

00:07:08.230 --> 00:07:12.440
will inherit the namespace's
default service account.

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:15.220
And currently, that namespace's
default service account

00:07:15.220 --> 00:07:19.070
usually gives you full
access to the cluster.

00:07:19.070 --> 00:07:22.210
So once your pod is running, it
has this access to the secret,

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:23.800
and that secret
will be then mounted

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:26.080
at a known location in
the pod's file system.

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:29.610
And so when you want to make
your request to the API server,

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:32.440
your pods can then
read in that jot.

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:34.270
They can use it
as a bearer token,

00:07:34.270 --> 00:07:36.730
and they make that request
to the Kubernetes API server,

00:07:36.730 --> 00:07:38.710
and therefore, they
will be authenticated.

00:07:38.710 --> 00:07:41.534
And of course, the API server
will allow you to do that.

00:07:41.534 --> 00:07:43.450
So again, if you wanted
a cron job or anything

00:07:43.450 --> 00:07:46.330
along those lines, maybe you
have a third-party resource

00:07:46.330 --> 00:07:50.290
that you want to extend the API
server with, you can do that,

00:07:50.290 --> 00:07:51.790
and the service
accounts provide you

00:07:51.790 --> 00:07:53.770
a mechanism for doing
that without having

00:07:53.770 --> 00:07:55.570
long-lived bearer
credentials that

00:07:55.570 --> 00:07:56.695
are outside of this system.

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:01.080
So that's authentication.

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:02.910
Let's talk about authorization.

00:08:02.910 --> 00:08:05.730
So given your identity,
or an identity,

00:08:05.730 --> 00:08:08.730
what can you do with it?

00:08:08.730 --> 00:08:11.815
Today, Kubernetes
supports what's called--

00:08:11.815 --> 00:08:15.630
at least called in Kubernetes
ABAC, Attribute-Based Access

00:08:15.630 --> 00:08:16.560
Control.

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:19.320
This is really just a very
simple file-based authorization

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:20.107
policy.

00:08:20.107 --> 00:08:21.690
If you're running
Kubernetes yourself,

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:23.398
you could configure
it with more details.

00:08:23.398 --> 00:08:25.650
But with Container
Engine today, it's

00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:28.650
a static file that basically
supports only an admin

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:30.750
identity.

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:34.320
What we'd like to do is offer
people more fine-grained access

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:35.100
control than that.

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:36.734
And so what we've
been doing is working

00:08:36.734 --> 00:08:38.400
with some of the
people in the community

00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:40.950
to get roles-based
access control, which

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:43.980
is another authorization
mechanism that the Kubernetes

00:08:43.980 --> 00:08:46.080
API server will support.

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:48.660
And this is going to allow
for a much fine-grained

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:49.752
authorization.

00:08:49.752 --> 00:08:51.210
And you can also
configure it using

00:08:51.210 --> 00:08:52.620
the Kubernetes API itself.

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:55.006
So the configuration
mechanism is through the API.

00:08:55.006 --> 00:08:56.880
You can create-- we'll
talk a little bit more

00:08:56.880 --> 00:08:59.617
about that in a minute-- roles,
and role bindings, and so on.

00:08:59.617 --> 00:09:01.200
And so that will
give you full control

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:05.350
to configure the
permissions on your cluster.

00:09:05.350 --> 00:09:08.550
So what is the
basic usage of RBAC?

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:10.260
If you look on
the left there, we

00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:12.630
have one of the resource
types, and Kubernetes

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:15.420
is a pod that might contain
multiple containers.

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:18.201
And the API server supports
two access mechanisms.

00:09:18.201 --> 00:09:19.950
You can call get on
the pod, and maybe you

00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:20.924
can call list on a pod.

00:09:20.924 --> 00:09:23.340
There are also a whole bunch
more, like create and delete.

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:26.160
But for this simple example,
we'll just focus on list

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:27.631
and get.

00:09:27.631 --> 00:09:29.130
And maybe on the
far right up there,

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:30.660
I've got the user in blue.

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:32.790
And I want to be able to
say, hey, this user should

00:09:32.790 --> 00:09:34.530
be able to call get on pods.

00:09:34.530 --> 00:09:38.280
They should be able to list
the pods in the system.

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:41.250
So how am I going to
do that with RBAC?

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:43.950
The first step is
to create a role.

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:45.060
That's the gray box.

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:47.250
We might create a
pod reader role.

00:09:47.250 --> 00:09:49.140
And this basically
says anyone who's

00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:52.050
a member of the pod reader
role has that capability.

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:53.610
They can call get on pods.

00:09:53.610 --> 00:09:57.780
They can call list on pods,
and life will be good.

00:09:57.780 --> 00:10:01.220
So I then need-- the final
piece of this is a role binding.

00:10:01.220 --> 00:10:05.790
That's the green box which
says this particular user is

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:07.530
bound to this particular role.

00:10:07.530 --> 00:10:09.690
If they have
authenticated themselves

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:11.850
as this particular
user, they have

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:14.080
this role available to them.

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:17.580
And so when the user comes
in, the authorization system

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:21.937
looks and says, OK, you're
alexmohr@google.com.

00:10:21.937 --> 00:10:22.770
Look at those roles.

00:10:22.770 --> 00:10:24.150
Yes, you do have
the pod reader role.

00:10:24.150 --> 00:10:25.691
There's a binding
there between them.

00:10:25.691 --> 00:10:27.270
You're trying to call pod.get.

00:10:27.270 --> 00:10:27.902
No problem.

00:10:27.902 --> 00:10:28.735
Everything succeeds.

00:10:31.270 --> 00:10:33.320
So that's the basic usage.

00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:35.610
RBAC supports a lot
more complexity,

00:10:35.610 --> 00:10:38.670
even in this case
if you said, well,

00:10:38.670 --> 00:10:40.750
what if I have nodes as well.

00:10:40.750 --> 00:10:44.670
Maybe I want to have, in the
bottom gray box over there,

00:10:44.670 --> 00:10:46.260
a node pod reader role.

00:10:46.260 --> 00:10:48.060
And that might allow
anyone with that role

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:53.450
to be able to both list pods as
well as get and list on nodes.

00:10:53.450 --> 00:10:57.100
And the bindings in this
case also can be extended.

00:10:57.100 --> 00:10:59.190
They don't just have
to be plain users.

00:10:59.190 --> 00:11:00.720
When your users
are authenticated,

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:03.210
they may come with a set of
groups associated with them.

00:11:03.210 --> 00:11:05.700
So if you're authenticated
as mohr@google.com,

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:08.370
and maybe I'm in a group--

00:11:08.370 --> 00:11:10.920
Cluster Admins, then
the Cluster Admin group

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:13.980
could be given the
node-pod-reader role.

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:16.590
And it's also not limited just
to users and groups, of course,

00:11:16.590 --> 00:11:20.039
but service accounts can
also be part of these things.

00:11:20.039 --> 00:11:22.080
And although I don't cover
it in the slides here,

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:24.900
there are many more forms of
this limited to name spaces.

00:11:24.900 --> 00:11:26.790
There are cluster
roles and so on.

00:11:26.790 --> 00:11:29.617
So it's a pretty decent
authorization system

00:11:29.617 --> 00:11:31.950
that gives you a lot of control
if you'd like to use it.

00:11:35.850 --> 00:11:36.780
There we go.

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:39.560
So the third pillar of IAM--

00:11:39.560 --> 00:11:41.240
we have authentication,
authorization.

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:44.660
The third A is often
referred to as auditing.

00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:47.960
So right now, the
cluster API is integrated

00:11:47.960 --> 00:11:49.570
with GCP audit logging.

00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:51.470
It's currently in beta.

00:11:51.470 --> 00:11:54.140
And we currently support
the admin activity form

00:11:54.140 --> 00:11:55.995
of this for cluster
modifications.

00:11:55.995 --> 00:11:57.620
So if you're going
to create a cluster,

00:11:57.620 --> 00:11:59.045
you're going to
upgrade a cluster,

00:11:59.045 --> 00:12:01.420
maybe you're going to change
the node pools in a cluster,

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:03.100
you're going to
delete a cluster--

00:12:03.100 --> 00:12:05.210
all of those kinds
of admin write

00:12:05.210 --> 00:12:09.590
actions are going to be
reflected in an audit log.

00:12:09.590 --> 00:12:11.030
And so it's more
or less what you

00:12:11.030 --> 00:12:12.860
would expect in simple form.

00:12:12.860 --> 00:12:14.990
Alice@example.com
created cluster foo.

00:12:14.990 --> 00:12:17.360
A Few lines later,
joe@example.com

00:12:17.360 --> 00:12:19.700
deleted the cluster bar.

00:12:19.700 --> 00:12:22.367
The details in here include
all of the request information.

00:12:22.367 --> 00:12:24.200
They include the response
that we sent back.

00:12:24.200 --> 00:12:28.250
They include the identity that
did it, time stamp, cluster IP,

00:12:28.250 --> 00:12:31.700
pretty rich set of metadata
about each of these requests.

00:12:31.700 --> 00:12:34.070
And those are then sent
to Stack Driver logging

00:12:34.070 --> 00:12:36.800
so you can go and use any of the
standard Stack Driver logging

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:39.830
APIs to go look through that.

00:12:39.830 --> 00:12:41.570
And of course, the
Google Cloud console

00:12:41.570 --> 00:12:47.080
also offers a relatively nice
overview where you can see,

00:12:47.080 --> 00:12:50.510
via the UI, what's been
going on in your clusters.

00:12:50.510 --> 00:12:53.780
Drill down and so on
is also available.

00:12:53.780 --> 00:12:55.520
On the bottom, data access--

00:12:55.520 --> 00:12:57.809
this is a very commonly
requested feature.

00:12:57.809 --> 00:13:00.350
You're starting to see more and
more of Google Cloud Platform

00:13:00.350 --> 00:13:02.210
products include data access.

00:13:02.210 --> 00:13:05.060
This would be a log of all of
the read operations being done.

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:08.687
So if somebody is calling a list
node pools or something like

00:13:08.687 --> 00:13:10.520
get cluster credentials,
those are the kinds

00:13:10.520 --> 00:13:13.670
of things you might expect to
see in the data access logs.

00:13:16.386 --> 00:13:17.760
And then finally
in this section,

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:19.472
I wanted to talk
briefly about secrets.

00:13:19.472 --> 00:13:20.930
So the previous
section was talking

00:13:20.930 --> 00:13:25.160
about authorizing to the
Kubernetes cluster itself.

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:28.490
What if I need to do something
like access a database,

00:13:28.490 --> 00:13:30.710
and I need a password
for that database,

00:13:30.710 --> 00:13:32.410
a username-password combo?

00:13:32.410 --> 00:13:35.390
Or what if I want to make
calls to GitHub and its API?

00:13:35.390 --> 00:13:38.412
Maybe I need an access
token in order to do that.

00:13:38.412 --> 00:13:40.370
So secrets are a really
nice Kubernetes feature

00:13:40.370 --> 00:13:41.990
that allow you to do that.

00:13:41.990 --> 00:13:44.480
You can store other static
credentials having nothing

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:47.900
to do with Kubernetes
in those secrets.

00:13:47.900 --> 00:13:50.330
You can then use
pods YAML in order

00:13:50.330 --> 00:13:52.400
to reference these secrets.

00:13:52.400 --> 00:13:54.860
And then when you actually
have these secrets in your pod,

00:13:54.860 --> 00:13:57.950
you can access them via a
file mount or an environment

00:13:57.950 --> 00:13:58.604
variable.

00:13:58.604 --> 00:14:00.020
And you can configure
how they get

00:14:00.020 --> 00:14:02.854
exposed to your app that runs.

00:14:02.854 --> 00:14:04.520
One of the nice things
about file mounts

00:14:04.520 --> 00:14:07.160
is that if you change
your secrets, eventually,

00:14:07.160 --> 00:14:09.300
the file mount will also
be updated to do it.

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:11.210
So if you have anything
that is rotating

00:14:11.210 --> 00:14:13.040
these things over
time, you can just

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:14.780
continually update the secret.

00:14:14.780 --> 00:14:16.970
And then based on
polling intervals

00:14:16.970 --> 00:14:19.867
and time to [INAUDIBLE]
on the kubelets, your app,

00:14:19.867 --> 00:14:21.950
as long as it's going to
be polling its file mount

00:14:21.950 --> 00:14:23.950
on a regular basis, should
pick up those changes

00:14:23.950 --> 00:14:25.790
as they happen.

00:14:25.790 --> 00:14:28.551
So to give you a brief example
of how that works, on the code

00:14:28.551 --> 00:14:30.050
snippets on the
upper right, you can

00:14:30.050 --> 00:14:33.170
see that the user name,
in this case, is admin.

00:14:33.170 --> 00:14:37.010
And Kubernetes YAML does
base 64 encoding for these,

00:14:37.010 --> 00:14:39.830
so that's the YWR string.

00:14:39.830 --> 00:14:43.150
Maybe we have a fun password
like 1f2d, et cetera,

00:14:43.150 --> 00:14:45.750
and that's also base 64 encoded.

00:14:45.750 --> 00:14:49.310
And so in order to specify
the secret, we specify the API

00:14:49.310 --> 00:14:53.360
version, the kind, metadata,
give it a name, My Secret,

00:14:53.360 --> 00:14:56.230
and then we pass in the data as
a username and password field.

00:14:59.760 --> 00:15:02.930
So that's all for IAM for now.

00:15:02.930 --> 00:15:04.650
I'm going to pass it
back to Jess, who's

00:15:04.650 --> 00:15:06.240
going to talk about
runtime security

00:15:06.240 --> 00:15:09.920
and give us a really neat demo.

00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:11.420
JESSICA FRAZELLE: Sweet.

00:15:11.420 --> 00:15:12.960
Yeah, so runtime security.

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:13.460
Yay.

00:15:13.460 --> 00:15:15.410
Containers.

00:15:15.410 --> 00:15:17.920
So I just wanted
to briefly cover

00:15:17.920 --> 00:15:20.990
the isolation primitives that
make up containers themselves.

00:15:20.990 --> 00:15:24.596
So if you don't know already,
containers aren't real things.

00:15:24.596 --> 00:15:25.970
When you create
a container, it's

00:15:25.970 --> 00:15:28.550
not actually creating this
thing that's called a container.

00:15:28.550 --> 00:15:31.460
It's using Linux
primitives that are

00:15:31.460 --> 00:15:34.190
namespaces and
cgroups to do this.

00:15:34.190 --> 00:15:36.950
Namespaces control
what a process can see,

00:15:36.950 --> 00:15:39.420
and cgroups control
what a process can use,

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:43.290
which is like a very clear
distinction between the two.

00:15:43.290 --> 00:15:45.410
So as far as namespaces
goes, there's

00:15:45.410 --> 00:15:47.750
a bunch that you can use.

00:15:47.750 --> 00:15:50.990
You can control what processes
are being seen, mounts,

00:15:50.990 --> 00:15:52.250
networks.

00:15:52.250 --> 00:15:54.060
UTS is hostname.

00:15:54.060 --> 00:15:56.900
IPC is messaging queues.

00:15:56.900 --> 00:16:01.230
User namespaces creates an
unprivileged user if you want,

00:16:01.230 --> 00:16:04.310
and it will be mapped
to a different user

00:16:04.310 --> 00:16:06.050
on your actual host.

00:16:06.050 --> 00:16:08.600
And then there's also
a cgroup namespace,

00:16:08.600 --> 00:16:12.140
just to confuse everyone,
which controls what

00:16:12.140 --> 00:16:14.270
cgroups you can actually see.

00:16:14.270 --> 00:16:16.940
As far as cgroups go,
you can control things

00:16:16.940 --> 00:16:21.050
like how much memory
will be used, CPU.

00:16:21.050 --> 00:16:25.190
You can set a container to
run on a specific CPU set.

00:16:25.190 --> 00:16:29.030
So if you want it to run
on 0 or 1, you can do that.

00:16:29.030 --> 00:16:31.640
You can control what
devices are being used,

00:16:31.640 --> 00:16:35.480
network priority, freezing
processes and threads,

00:16:35.480 --> 00:16:39.290
and block I/O, so writing
to disk bandwidth and stuff

00:16:39.290 --> 00:16:39.790
like that.

00:16:39.790 --> 00:16:42.050
So there's really
a lot of leverage

00:16:42.050 --> 00:16:45.950
for you to take the
groundwork here and then

00:16:45.950 --> 00:16:50.480
build on top of them with more
hardening aspects of Linux,

00:16:50.480 --> 00:16:51.980
which we'll cover now.

00:16:51.980 --> 00:16:54.190
So one of these is AppArmor.

00:16:54.190 --> 00:16:57.980
And this is a Linux
security module.

00:16:57.980 --> 00:17:02.990
This is super helpful
for creating profiles

00:17:02.990 --> 00:17:06.200
that will define what
your process wants

00:17:06.200 --> 00:17:10.609
to write to so you can set
certain files as read only

00:17:10.609 --> 00:17:13.500
without setting the entire mount
as read only, which is insanely

00:17:13.500 --> 00:17:14.000
helpful.

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:17.839
Because read only file
systems are great,

00:17:17.839 --> 00:17:21.692
but it's very hard to make an
actual application run entirely

00:17:21.692 --> 00:17:22.400
that's read only.

00:17:22.400 --> 00:17:24.191
You're going to need
to write to something.

00:17:24.191 --> 00:17:26.640
So AppArmor is great for that.

00:17:26.640 --> 00:17:31.240
You can also deny system
functions, like Mount,

00:17:31.240 --> 00:17:33.260
net raw, stuff like that.

00:17:33.260 --> 00:17:35.720
It doesn't cover all
system functions.

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:38.180
There's only a small subset.

00:17:38.180 --> 00:17:41.300
And we're going to push
towards same defaults.

00:17:41.300 --> 00:17:43.816
Docker has the same default
that prevents writing to PROC,

00:17:43.816 --> 00:17:45.440
prevents writing to
SYS, all the things

00:17:45.440 --> 00:17:47.210
that you should not
be doing regardless,

00:17:47.210 --> 00:17:51.000
unless you are running
as a privileged container

00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:52.370
and preventing mount.

00:17:52.370 --> 00:17:54.710
So this is beta in Kubernetes.

00:17:54.710 --> 00:17:57.920
And if you want to use it,
you can do the following

00:17:57.920 --> 00:18:01.760
and create an annotation
for your container

00:18:01.760 --> 00:18:03.550
that points to this.

00:18:03.550 --> 00:18:06.270
And what we're doing here is
using the runtime default,

00:18:06.270 --> 00:18:08.120
which will then use
the Docker default

00:18:08.120 --> 00:18:11.650
profile for your basic NGINX
container that we have here.

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:17.930
So moving on-- another
one of these is seccomp.

00:18:17.930 --> 00:18:19.820
And seccomp gives
you more leverage

00:18:19.820 --> 00:18:21.800
over all of the sys calls.

00:18:21.800 --> 00:18:24.320
So AppArmor, you could
do, if you like, mount.

00:18:24.320 --> 00:18:27.940
But seccomp, you can define
a whitelist or blacklist

00:18:27.940 --> 00:18:31.470
of sys calls that your
application is going to use.

00:18:31.470 --> 00:18:35.390
So in Docker, there
is a waitlist,

00:18:35.390 --> 00:18:38.510
and it has some really
[? sane ?] defaults.

00:18:38.510 --> 00:18:40.880
When we were working
on writing it,

00:18:40.880 --> 00:18:44.250
I went through all the past
CBEs that were super bad.

00:18:44.250 --> 00:18:46.640
And we blocked
things like P trace,

00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:49.130
and cloning new
unprivileged namespaces,

00:18:49.130 --> 00:18:53.550
which has the highest CVE rate
for breaking out of containers,

00:18:53.550 --> 00:18:57.590
things that if your application
is not running as privileged,

00:18:57.590 --> 00:19:00.990
you probably don't need anyways.

00:19:00.990 --> 00:19:04.550
So for 95% of these
cases, it works out

00:19:04.550 --> 00:19:06.030
of the box, which is great.

00:19:06.030 --> 00:19:07.940
So this is alpha in Kubernetes.

00:19:07.940 --> 00:19:10.250
So if you want to
use this, it's kind

00:19:10.250 --> 00:19:11.960
of the same thing as before.

00:19:11.960 --> 00:19:14.570
You set the annotation
on your container.

00:19:14.570 --> 00:19:18.000
And for this one, we're
using Docker default profile.

00:19:20.670 --> 00:19:22.110
So there's a bunch
of other things

00:19:22.110 --> 00:19:24.240
that you can set as well.

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:28.170
You can set SELinux context
for labels on volumes

00:19:28.170 --> 00:19:29.680
that you have in
your containers.

00:19:29.680 --> 00:19:32.970
You can run all your containers
in a pod as a non-root user

00:19:32.970 --> 00:19:35.280
or ensure that that's happening.

00:19:35.280 --> 00:19:38.460
You can set a read only root
file system, which like I said,

00:19:38.460 --> 00:19:40.380
if that's impossible
for your application,

00:19:40.380 --> 00:19:44.560
you can actually define
granularly these things

00:19:44.560 --> 00:19:47.630
in AppArmor profile instead.

00:19:47.630 --> 00:19:50.790
And then you can also
add or drop capabilities

00:19:50.790 --> 00:19:54.950
to your containers if
you do not need them.

00:19:54.950 --> 00:19:57.860
So what this looks like if
you want to say drop net raw,

00:19:57.860 --> 00:19:59.450
which really isn't
all that useful

00:19:59.450 --> 00:20:01.730
unless you want to use ping--

00:20:01.730 --> 00:20:04.220
you could do this in
the security context

00:20:04.220 --> 00:20:05.940
for each container.

00:20:05.940 --> 00:20:09.260
And then you can also set
SELinux options and labels,

00:20:09.260 --> 00:20:11.300
like I said, on your containers.

00:20:11.300 --> 00:20:14.090
You can define that you want
it to be a read only root file

00:20:14.090 --> 00:20:15.084
system.

00:20:15.084 --> 00:20:17.000
And you can also make
sure that all containers

00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:21.360
are running as a non-root user.

00:20:21.360 --> 00:20:23.780
So now we're going to
do a demo and switch

00:20:23.780 --> 00:20:26.600
to the other computer.

00:20:26.600 --> 00:20:27.250
Cool.

00:20:27.250 --> 00:20:27.770
OK.

00:20:27.770 --> 00:20:30.170
So this will be fun.

00:20:30.170 --> 00:20:33.490
So I have a GKE
cluster that I spun up

00:20:33.490 --> 00:20:37.940
that's running in alpha mode
so that we can use seccomp,

00:20:37.940 --> 00:20:40.820
so you can do the
same if you want.

00:20:40.820 --> 00:20:45.732
So we'll just get
another terminal

00:20:45.732 --> 00:20:46.690
since this one is dead.

00:20:58.530 --> 00:20:59.180
Cool.

00:20:59.180 --> 00:21:00.600
Well, that was fun.

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:05.850
So we'll just show that there
are no pods currently running.

00:21:05.850 --> 00:21:06.491
Fresh cluster.

00:21:06.491 --> 00:21:06.990
Cool.

00:21:06.990 --> 00:21:09.630
So now we can have
our way with this.

00:21:09.630 --> 00:21:13.830
So I have, first
off, this fun pod

00:21:13.830 --> 00:21:23.070
that we're going to
spin up that is cool.

00:21:23.070 --> 00:21:30.070
So if we look at
our first pod, it's

00:21:30.070 --> 00:21:32.380
going to create five replicas.

00:21:32.380 --> 00:21:34.930
If we look here, it's
just a simple app named

00:21:34.930 --> 00:21:38.000
Echo, which opens up NGINX.

00:21:38.000 --> 00:21:41.050
And NGINX just echoes out a
bunch of header information

00:21:41.050 --> 00:21:42.980
and the body of the request.

00:21:42.980 --> 00:21:46.120
And it's just a stupid demo app.

00:21:46.120 --> 00:21:49.510
Then we have our NGINX configs,
and we're exposing port 80.

00:21:49.510 --> 00:21:54.190
And we're not doing anything in
this example with annotations

00:21:54.190 --> 00:21:58.300
in seccomp, I just want to show
that we can do things that we

00:21:58.300 --> 00:21:59.800
should not be able to do.

00:21:59.800 --> 00:22:04.920
So we'll spin this one
up with kubectl create.

00:22:10.590 --> 00:22:16.210
So we'll just see that
our pods are running.

00:22:16.210 --> 00:22:20.590
And I'll just grab one
of these random ones.

00:22:20.590 --> 00:22:21.840
And we can exec in.

00:22:28.378 --> 00:22:33.130
And then I'll set the container
as echo, and we'll run bash.

00:22:33.130 --> 00:22:34.420
So now we're in.

00:22:34.420 --> 00:22:35.070
Hacker voice.

00:22:35.070 --> 00:22:36.400
Just kidding.

00:22:36.400 --> 00:22:40.660
So I'm going to try
to do something that--

00:22:40.660 --> 00:22:45.580
if this was running an NGINX
container, and I was actually--

00:22:45.580 --> 00:22:47.770
escaped the
application, if I tried

00:22:47.770 --> 00:22:52.750
to like unshare something like
a user namespace to break out,

00:22:52.750 --> 00:22:54.160
we would not want
this to happen.

00:22:54.160 --> 00:22:57.190
But of course, it works, because
nothing is being blocked.

00:22:57.190 --> 00:22:59.970
I'm not running
with any seccomp in.

00:22:59.970 --> 00:23:00.990
Kind of blows.

00:23:00.990 --> 00:23:06.030
So that's what without seccomp,
If we look at this other one

00:23:06.030 --> 00:23:10.650
that I have that actually
does add a seccomp profile,

00:23:10.650 --> 00:23:13.680
it's the exact same as
our Echo application.

00:23:13.680 --> 00:23:15.680
This one I just named NGINX.

00:23:15.680 --> 00:23:18.660
There's five replicas again,
because it's a cluster.

00:23:18.660 --> 00:23:20.850
Might as well use it.

00:23:20.850 --> 00:23:25.290
And we're setting Docker
Default as our seccomp profile.

00:23:25.290 --> 00:23:30.300
So by default, we
know, since I wrote it,

00:23:30.300 --> 00:23:32.760
that unshare in a
new user namespace

00:23:32.760 --> 00:23:33.840
is going to be blocked.

00:23:33.840 --> 00:23:37.180
So let's just verify
that by spinning this up

00:23:37.180 --> 00:23:38.800
with another kubectl create.

00:23:43.810 --> 00:23:45.708
We'll make sure
that these are up.

00:23:52.410 --> 00:23:54.810
And I'll just grab another
one of these randomly,

00:23:54.810 --> 00:23:55.595
and we'll exec in.

00:24:01.656 --> 00:24:05.050
The container names in
NGINX, and bash again.

00:24:05.050 --> 00:24:10.570
So now we'll unshare and
be evil, and it's denied.

00:24:10.570 --> 00:24:13.090
So that just basically
verifies that it works,

00:24:13.090 --> 00:24:14.680
but that was no fun, basically.

00:24:14.680 --> 00:24:17.140
So let's go into a
more real world example

00:24:17.140 --> 00:24:19.930
here of trying to
stop a bad person.

00:24:19.930 --> 00:24:24.340
So I have another cluster here
that I'm going to switch to.

00:24:28.590 --> 00:24:32.050
And on this cluster, it just
so happens that sysadmin

00:24:32.050 --> 00:24:35.070
me forgot to upgrade
one of the nodes

00:24:35.070 --> 00:24:37.950
after the Dirty
COW vulnerability.

00:24:37.950 --> 00:24:41.580
So the interesting
thing about Dirty COW,

00:24:41.580 --> 00:24:43.200
if you all didn't know--

00:24:43.200 --> 00:24:45.349
when I first heard
about it, what

00:24:45.349 --> 00:24:47.140
I'll do with these
types of vulnerabilities

00:24:47.140 --> 00:24:52.020
is I'll try and make sure that
the Docker set comp profile

00:24:52.020 --> 00:24:54.870
either prevents it or that
we can edit it in such a way

00:24:54.870 --> 00:24:57.090
that it will be prevented
for everyone that uses it.

00:24:57.090 --> 00:24:59.280
So when I first heard
about Dirty COW,

00:24:59.280 --> 00:25:02.460
I was like, crap,
that one is not

00:25:02.460 --> 00:25:04.590
actually prevented
by the default.

00:25:04.590 --> 00:25:06.660
Because it's using madvise.

00:25:06.660 --> 00:25:09.180
It's using literally
nothing bad.

00:25:09.180 --> 00:25:13.080
You're just writing to
a read only memory map

00:25:13.080 --> 00:25:15.780
and using all the sys cals
that people use daily,

00:25:15.780 --> 00:25:17.250
which is hard.

00:25:17.250 --> 00:25:21.000
But what I didn't know
was that it needed--

00:25:21.000 --> 00:25:21.720
if we look here--

00:25:25.430 --> 00:25:28.790
you needed madvise, and you
needed madvise don't need.

00:25:28.790 --> 00:25:33.750
So I was thinking to myself
let's just not allow that.

00:25:33.750 --> 00:25:35.810
We'll allow
everything of madvise

00:25:35.810 --> 00:25:38.210
but not madvise don't need.

00:25:38.210 --> 00:25:40.970
So I had this
patch ready to send

00:25:40.970 --> 00:25:45.500
like when the vulnerability was
going to be publicly disclosed,

00:25:45.500 --> 00:25:47.210
and I was just waiting on it.

00:25:47.210 --> 00:25:49.647
And it turns out, actually--

00:25:49.647 --> 00:25:51.230
I didn't realize
this before the patch

00:25:51.230 --> 00:25:53.750
was made, which was good--

00:25:53.750 --> 00:25:58.500
glibc malloc uses madvise don't
need literally all the time.

00:25:58.500 --> 00:26:02.540
So that would break almost
all applications by default.

00:26:02.540 --> 00:26:04.200
It could not be a
default for anyone.

00:26:04.200 --> 00:26:06.650
So this one is very troublesome.

00:26:06.650 --> 00:26:10.560
So if sysadmin me is
thinking about my cluster,

00:26:10.560 --> 00:26:14.210
and for some reason, we don't
use glibc for any of it,

00:26:14.210 --> 00:26:18.470
I could be like, OK, I'm
going to, for the time being,

00:26:18.470 --> 00:26:21.200
before I upgrade all the
nodes, and before I realize

00:26:21.200 --> 00:26:25.430
if we've been affected
by this or anyone got in,

00:26:25.430 --> 00:26:29.610
I'm going to deploy all these
pods with this custom set

00:26:29.610 --> 00:26:33.410
comp profile like I showed
that takes madvise don't need.

00:26:33.410 --> 00:26:37.310
It allows literally all of
madvise but madvise don't need.

00:26:37.310 --> 00:26:40.400
So the order of operations
I'm going to show these

00:26:40.400 --> 00:26:42.980
is in a specific order,
because after I pwn the node,

00:26:42.980 --> 00:26:45.800
then I can't spin out
more containers on it.

00:26:45.800 --> 00:26:48.740
So we're first going
to show blocking it.

00:26:48.740 --> 00:26:55.240
So sysadmin me takes
my pod spec here.

00:26:55.240 --> 00:26:59.650
And I'm like, I'm going to use
this custom profile that I put.

00:26:59.650 --> 00:27:03.310
So it's basically a fork of
the default that adds that.

00:27:03.310 --> 00:27:06.400
And I'm going to run
this pop container, which

00:27:06.400 --> 00:27:10.990
basically tries to exploit
the Dirty COW vulnerability.

00:27:10.990 --> 00:27:13.720
And since I labeled my nodes--

00:27:13.720 --> 00:27:15.730
which in real life,
the sysadmin probably

00:27:15.730 --> 00:27:18.240
didn't, but I did for the
sake of this demo to make sure

00:27:18.240 --> 00:27:20.850
it goes on the bad node--

00:27:20.850 --> 00:27:23.320
this is going to run
on the Dirty COW node.

00:27:23.320 --> 00:27:24.830
So if we spin this one up--

00:27:35.010 --> 00:27:36.604
and now let's get our pods--

00:27:40.780 --> 00:27:41.850
cool.

00:27:41.850 --> 00:27:46.425
So let's grab this
one and get some logs.

00:27:52.710 --> 00:27:55.440
So it tried to pwn the
machine, and it was like,

00:27:55.440 --> 00:27:58.960
could not pwn, and now our sad
hacker is sad, which is great.

00:27:58.960 --> 00:27:59.940
So we prevented that.

00:27:59.940 --> 00:28:03.495
It gave the sysadmin time
to upgrade stuff or assess

00:28:03.495 --> 00:28:06.840
if we had been
affected, which is cool.

00:28:06.840 --> 00:28:10.710
So another sysadmin comes
along, maybe also me,

00:28:10.710 --> 00:28:13.290
and forgets to do this--

00:28:13.290 --> 00:28:15.170
forgets to add the
seccomp profile,

00:28:15.170 --> 00:28:17.040
forgets to even write
the seccomp profile,

00:28:17.040 --> 00:28:18.750
just does not care.

00:28:18.750 --> 00:28:22.639
So we have another
one of these where

00:28:22.639 --> 00:28:23.930
it's going to run on Dirty COW.

00:28:23.930 --> 00:28:26.070
It's going to run the
same exact container pop,

00:28:26.070 --> 00:28:28.580
and we're not going
to have any seccomp.

00:28:28.580 --> 00:28:31.970
I had to do this one second,
because the node literally

00:28:31.970 --> 00:28:33.680
will not respond after this.

00:28:33.680 --> 00:28:37.495
So let's delete the old.

00:28:46.710 --> 00:28:48.160
And now we'll create the new.

00:28:58.000 --> 00:28:59.155
And now let's get our pods.

00:29:02.950 --> 00:29:03.970
Cool.

00:29:03.970 --> 00:29:05.920
And now we'll grab the
logs from this one.

00:29:13.940 --> 00:29:15.230
And so game over.

00:29:15.230 --> 00:29:17.600
We could write to this
read only memory map.

00:29:17.600 --> 00:29:20.660
We had all these madvise
calls just totally

00:29:20.660 --> 00:29:22.010
killing the node itself.

00:29:22.010 --> 00:29:24.620
So that's the sad story.

00:29:24.620 --> 00:29:28.340
And then also, just
for fun, we can spin up

00:29:28.340 --> 00:29:30.121
one of these on the
non-Dirty-COW node.

00:29:30.121 --> 00:29:32.120
And I'll show you that
it also doesn't work just

00:29:32.120 --> 00:29:35.340
to verify that I'm not
completely fooling you all.

00:29:42.960 --> 00:29:55.690
So if we delete the
old, create the new,

00:29:55.690 --> 00:29:57.999
we'll grab one of these
again, and our sad hacker

00:29:57.999 --> 00:29:59.040
is going to be sad again.

00:30:11.560 --> 00:30:12.600
Cool.

00:30:12.600 --> 00:30:14.977
So now if we switch
back to the slides,

00:30:14.977 --> 00:30:16.560
basically, what we
learned is that you

00:30:16.560 --> 00:30:17.920
can run custom profiles.

00:30:17.920 --> 00:30:19.860
You can stop bad
things from happening

00:30:19.860 --> 00:30:22.290
if you know that they're there.

00:30:22.290 --> 00:30:25.679
Usually, the default
Docker profile

00:30:25.679 --> 00:30:26.720
would block these things.

00:30:26.720 --> 00:30:29.700
There's actually a page that I
wrote in the Docker repo that

00:30:29.700 --> 00:30:33.570
has 20-plus vulnerabilities
that were stopped by the default

00:30:33.570 --> 00:30:34.170
profile.

00:30:34.170 --> 00:30:38.100
But this was just a fun example
for something that was not.

00:30:38.100 --> 00:30:40.740
And in other cases, I
would try, like I said,

00:30:40.740 --> 00:30:44.190
to modify the profile
without breaking every one,

00:30:44.190 --> 00:30:46.760
which in this
case, it cannot be.

00:30:46.760 --> 00:30:51.140
So the cool thing about
this is that in GKE,

00:30:51.140 --> 00:30:55.610
if you run with opt in for
automatic upgrades for GKE node

00:30:55.610 --> 00:30:58.340
pools, this would never
happen, because we control

00:30:58.340 --> 00:31:01.320
the OS for all the nodes.

00:31:01.320 --> 00:31:04.442
So that's definitely something
you should think about,

00:31:04.442 --> 00:31:05.900
because we know
about these things.

00:31:05.900 --> 00:31:07.620
We upgrade the nodes for you.

00:31:07.620 --> 00:31:09.290
And then you don't
even have to worry--

00:31:09.290 --> 00:31:10.730
did I miss a node?

00:31:10.730 --> 00:31:13.910
Do I need to do some sort of
custom profile, all of that?

00:31:13.910 --> 00:31:15.110
It's pretty great.

00:31:15.110 --> 00:31:18.790
And other great features of
the OS that we use are it's

00:31:18.790 --> 00:31:20.270
a super minimal base.

00:31:20.270 --> 00:31:23.630
Really, everything has
to be running containers.

00:31:23.630 --> 00:31:26.810
There's a firewall that
drops all network connections

00:31:26.810 --> 00:31:30.020
except for SSH by default.

00:31:30.020 --> 00:31:33.290
It's the default node
image for all GKE.

00:31:33.290 --> 00:31:35.420
And it's based off Chrome
OS, which has really

00:31:35.420 --> 00:31:40.200
been tried and tested as a
great security operating system.

00:31:40.200 --> 00:31:43.110
The entire file system
is mounted as read only,

00:31:43.110 --> 00:31:47.120
and then certain file
mounts are remounted again.

00:31:47.120 --> 00:31:50.750
Like var/lib/docker needs to
be executable and writable,

00:31:50.750 --> 00:31:54.260
and your home and var
and a staple partition

00:31:54.260 --> 00:31:57.300
mount can hold persisted
state over time.

00:31:57.300 --> 00:31:59.720
But that's like literally it.

00:31:59.720 --> 00:32:03.140
So super great that GKE
could possibly never

00:32:03.140 --> 00:32:04.839
have this happen.

00:32:04.839 --> 00:32:06.380
Now I'm going to
pass it back to Alex

00:32:06.380 --> 00:32:08.595
to cover the roadmap
for the future.

00:32:08.595 --> 00:32:09.720
ALEX MOHR: Thank you, Jess.

00:32:09.720 --> 00:32:10.694
Yeah.

00:32:10.694 --> 00:32:13.129
[APPLAUSE]

00:32:17.520 --> 00:32:19.020
So for the last
section, I'd like

00:32:19.020 --> 00:32:22.200
to talk a little bit about the
Kubernetes security roadmap.

00:32:22.200 --> 00:32:24.630
Full disclaimer-- this is
an open source project,

00:32:24.630 --> 00:32:27.030
and Google Container
Engine inherits whatever

00:32:27.030 --> 00:32:28.980
Kubernetes upstream does.

00:32:28.980 --> 00:32:30.780
We obviously don't
control it, so these

00:32:30.780 --> 00:32:34.350
are somewhat speculative
future directions.

00:32:34.350 --> 00:32:36.930
Many people of the
community share these goals,

00:32:36.930 --> 00:32:39.870
but there's no guarantee as
to what the future will bring,

00:32:39.870 --> 00:32:41.426
of course.

00:32:41.426 --> 00:32:42.300
So two parts to this.

00:32:42.300 --> 00:32:43.758
First, I'm going
to talk about some

00:32:43.758 --> 00:32:45.870
of the more long-term
goals that we have,

00:32:45.870 --> 00:32:47.520
the problems that
we'd like to solve.

00:32:47.520 --> 00:32:49.103
And then the second
half of this, I'll

00:32:49.103 --> 00:32:52.050
talk about what we might expect
to land with Kubernetes 1.6,

00:32:52.050 --> 00:32:56.290
1.7, 1.8 over the
next six months.

00:32:56.290 --> 00:32:58.740
So we talked a little bit about
security model and stages.

00:32:58.740 --> 00:33:01.110
Jess gave us a nice
introduction to single tenant

00:33:01.110 --> 00:33:02.640
with multiple
identities, and then

00:33:02.640 --> 00:33:06.390
this notion of cooperative
soft multi-tenancy.

00:33:06.390 --> 00:33:07.920
What we'd like to
see, though, is

00:33:07.920 --> 00:33:10.140
hard multi-tenancy over time.

00:33:10.140 --> 00:33:12.360
So this would be where
those security boundaries

00:33:12.360 --> 00:33:15.090
between the multiple tenants
of a cluster become much more

00:33:15.090 --> 00:33:16.470
strongly enforced.

00:33:16.470 --> 00:33:18.820
And I'll talk a little bit
about that in a moment.

00:33:18.820 --> 00:33:21.486
We might also want to see things
like better resource isolation.

00:33:21.486 --> 00:33:24.030
If I have two containers
running in different processes

00:33:24.030 --> 00:33:26.580
on the same machine,
maybe one of them

00:33:26.580 --> 00:33:29.640
ends up trashing my L2 cache,
and that causes the other one

00:33:29.640 --> 00:33:30.690
to run more slowly.

00:33:30.690 --> 00:33:32.190
So there are some
interesting things

00:33:32.190 --> 00:33:33.900
in terms of looking at
cycles per instruction

00:33:33.900 --> 00:33:35.100
that you might
want to do in terms

00:33:35.100 --> 00:33:36.930
of trying to get better
resource isolation

00:33:36.930 --> 00:33:39.972
between these different systems.

00:33:39.972 --> 00:33:42.180
If you want to think about
what would that maybe look

00:33:42.180 --> 00:33:46.100
like, we'll know that we've
met the hard multi-tenancy goal

00:33:46.100 --> 00:33:47.850
if you were a provider
and you said, yeah,

00:33:47.850 --> 00:33:50.250
I'm happy to have
somebody from Company A

00:33:50.250 --> 00:33:52.815
give me some code, whatever
it is, and I'll run it.

00:33:52.815 --> 00:33:55.190
And Company B gives me some
other code, and we'll run it,

00:33:55.190 --> 00:33:57.640
and they're running on the
same system, the same cluster,

00:33:57.640 --> 00:33:58.920
the same nodes.

00:33:58.920 --> 00:34:01.470
So if you had hard
multi-tenancy,

00:34:01.470 --> 00:34:04.117
it's really about
this hard isolation.

00:34:04.117 --> 00:34:05.700
And just because you
don't necessarily

00:34:05.700 --> 00:34:07.866
want to run third-party
code for multiple companies,

00:34:07.866 --> 00:34:10.830
this might also be useful to
you inside your organization.

00:34:10.830 --> 00:34:12.940
Maybe you have multiple
different departments.

00:34:12.940 --> 00:34:14.730
Maybe you have multiple teams.

00:34:14.730 --> 00:34:18.710
This kind of thing might
also help in that case.

00:34:18.710 --> 00:34:21.370
So Jess talked to us about
a cluster and the project

00:34:21.370 --> 00:34:23.920
as a security boundary
and gave you this slide

00:34:23.920 --> 00:34:25.259
that you saw earlier.

00:34:25.259 --> 00:34:26.300
We'd like to change this.

00:34:26.300 --> 00:34:28.870
Instead of everything being the
same authenticated identity--

00:34:28.870 --> 00:34:30.790
the pods having the
same permissions--

00:34:30.790 --> 00:34:32.590
what we'd like to see
instead is something

00:34:32.590 --> 00:34:35.770
where the namespace and pod
become much more of a boundary

00:34:35.770 --> 00:34:37.171
than they are today.

00:34:37.171 --> 00:34:39.670
So this would mean things like
the authorization permissions

00:34:39.670 --> 00:34:41.914
could be granted at a
namespace resource level.

00:34:41.914 --> 00:34:43.330
So if we look at
the example here,

00:34:43.330 --> 00:34:45.306
you might see Team
1 has a namespace,

00:34:45.306 --> 00:34:47.139
and they're running
their front ends in red.

00:34:47.139 --> 00:34:49.780
Their databases are in yellow.

00:34:49.780 --> 00:34:51.280
Maybe there's
another team, Team 2.

00:34:51.280 --> 00:34:53.770
They've got their own setup
with front ends and databases,

00:34:53.770 --> 00:34:56.260
shown below in blue and green.

00:34:56.260 --> 00:34:59.710
And the three nodes here
are hosting the same pods

00:34:59.710 --> 00:35:02.290
as before, but now we
have better notions

00:35:02.290 --> 00:35:03.904
of separation between them.

00:35:03.904 --> 00:35:05.320
In this case, maybe
the nodes have

00:35:05.320 --> 00:35:07.990
their individual identities
and per-node permissions.

00:35:07.990 --> 00:35:10.480
The pods have identity with
fine-grained permissions.

00:35:10.480 --> 00:35:12.730
And we could even maybe think
about how the pods could

00:35:12.730 --> 00:35:14.560
have limited network
access to just

00:35:14.560 --> 00:35:16.415
whatever is necessary for them.

00:35:16.415 --> 00:35:18.040
So let's walk through
these four things

00:35:18.040 --> 00:35:19.570
and talk about
the user scenarios

00:35:19.570 --> 00:35:21.760
that those would reflect.

00:35:21.760 --> 00:35:25.060
So authorization permissions at
the namespace resource level--

00:35:25.060 --> 00:35:27.520
so we'd like to move that to
the per namespace per resource

00:35:27.520 --> 00:35:28.750
permissions.

00:35:28.750 --> 00:35:31.260
Today, everything
has root in cluster.

00:35:31.260 --> 00:35:33.010
And that means that
cluster is a boundary.

00:35:33.010 --> 00:35:36.280
Instead, what we'd like to do is
take advantage of this security

00:35:36.280 --> 00:35:37.900
principle, least privilege.

00:35:37.900 --> 00:35:41.020
Should be familiar to
many people in security.

00:35:41.020 --> 00:35:42.670
And so the users and
the system should

00:35:42.670 --> 00:35:45.610
have the least privilege
possible, also the workloads.

00:35:45.610 --> 00:35:48.499
So for example, if one user
of the cluster is Alice,

00:35:48.499 --> 00:35:50.290
she would like to be--
we want to make sure

00:35:50.290 --> 00:35:53.980
that she can list the
engineering services,

00:35:53.980 --> 00:35:55.690
because she's a
member of that team.

00:35:55.690 --> 00:35:58.540
But she shouldn't be able to
go and pull down HR profiles,

00:35:58.540 --> 00:36:00.490
offer letters, things like that.

00:36:00.490 --> 00:36:03.310
Maybe Bob can create things
in the test namespace,

00:36:03.310 --> 00:36:05.830
because he's a member
of the testing team,

00:36:05.830 --> 00:36:07.930
but he shouldn't be able
to access things in prod

00:36:07.930 --> 00:36:10.870
unless he's a member of your
on-call rotation, for example.

00:36:10.870 --> 00:36:12.534
Or maybe your
scheduler needs to be

00:36:12.534 --> 00:36:14.200
able to know what the
state of the nodes

00:36:14.200 --> 00:36:16.120
are and the state of the
pods are in the system,

00:36:16.120 --> 00:36:17.994
but there's really no
need for your scheduler

00:36:17.994 --> 00:36:20.470
to be able to read
your HTTPS certificate,

00:36:20.470 --> 00:36:22.885
so it should not be able
to read the secrets file.

00:36:25.536 --> 00:36:26.910
As far as the
nodes go, we'd like

00:36:26.910 --> 00:36:28.770
to move them to have
per-node identity

00:36:28.770 --> 00:36:30.360
and per-node permissions.

00:36:30.360 --> 00:36:32.580
So right now, each of
your nodes can read any

00:36:32.580 --> 00:36:33.840
of the secrets in your system.

00:36:33.840 --> 00:36:37.440
They can read any of
the pods in the system.

00:36:37.440 --> 00:36:39.690
That's OK for today, but
we'd like to really move this

00:36:39.690 --> 00:36:40.960
to the least privilege model.

00:36:40.960 --> 00:36:43.530
So in this case, some example
might be if I have a node,

00:36:43.530 --> 00:36:45.060
it should only be
able to read data

00:36:45.060 --> 00:36:47.272
about pods that are
scheduled on that node.

00:36:47.272 --> 00:36:49.480
If a pod is running on some
other node in the system,

00:36:49.480 --> 00:36:52.317
there's really no need for
it to be able to read that.

00:36:52.317 --> 00:36:54.150
Similarly, it should
be able to read secrets

00:36:54.150 --> 00:36:55.986
for the things
running on itself,

00:36:55.986 --> 00:36:58.110
but it shouldn't be able
to read secrets for things

00:36:58.110 --> 00:37:00.580
that it doesn't
need to know about.

00:37:00.580 --> 00:37:02.880
And if we get to this point,
then, a compromised node

00:37:02.880 --> 00:37:04.920
will not allow any
additional escalation

00:37:04.920 --> 00:37:06.045
through the Kubernetes API.

00:37:06.045 --> 00:37:07.545
You'll be able to
access things that

00:37:07.545 --> 00:37:08.770
happen to live on that node.

00:37:08.770 --> 00:37:10.770
But if there are 500 other
nodes in your system,

00:37:10.770 --> 00:37:14.994
it won't be able to do
things that reflect on them.

00:37:14.994 --> 00:37:17.160
Third, we would like pods
to have their own identity

00:37:17.160 --> 00:37:17.950
and permissions.

00:37:17.950 --> 00:37:20.850
So as I mentioned now, if you
have a default service account,

00:37:20.850 --> 00:37:23.160
the default service account
has full admin capability

00:37:23.160 --> 00:37:24.632
on the cluster.

00:37:24.632 --> 00:37:26.340
Instead, we would like
to move to a model

00:37:26.340 --> 00:37:30.386
where you have to actively
grant workloads permissions.

00:37:30.386 --> 00:37:32.010
So if you want to
create your cron job,

00:37:32.010 --> 00:37:34.230
it would be reasonable to
say yes, my cron job should

00:37:34.230 --> 00:37:36.510
be able to create
pods in the system,

00:37:36.510 --> 00:37:39.450
or yes, whatever
my other extension

00:37:39.450 --> 00:37:41.947
is that's going to
collect statistics

00:37:41.947 --> 00:37:43.530
about the things
running on the system

00:37:43.530 --> 00:37:44.988
and create a
dashboard that lets me

00:37:44.988 --> 00:37:47.160
do some amount of
visualization, it should also

00:37:47.160 --> 00:37:48.780
be able to have these things.

00:37:48.780 --> 00:37:51.275
But if it's just your NGINX
running on your cluster,

00:37:51.275 --> 00:37:53.400
NGINX really should not be
able to go in and delete

00:37:53.400 --> 00:37:58.630
all of your pods, your
deployments, and so on.

00:37:58.630 --> 00:38:00.480
Similarly, if you
have namespaces,

00:38:00.480 --> 00:38:03.060
if you're running a pod in
one particular namespace,

00:38:03.060 --> 00:38:05.380
maybe it can access things
within that localized

00:38:05.380 --> 00:38:08.640
namespace, but other namespaces
that are isolated from it

00:38:08.640 --> 00:38:12.020
it should not be able
to have access to.

00:38:12.020 --> 00:38:13.756
And then fourth,
network policy--

00:38:13.756 --> 00:38:16.130
so we'd like to move from all
workloads receiving traffic

00:38:16.130 --> 00:38:19.670
from anywhere on the network to
having network control policy--

00:38:19.670 --> 00:38:22.520
sorry, network connectivity
controllable by policy.

00:38:22.520 --> 00:38:25.400
So this case would be where you
have a front end system, maybe

00:38:25.400 --> 00:38:26.570
a back end system.

00:38:26.570 --> 00:38:28.820
The front end should be able
to talk to the back ends,

00:38:28.820 --> 00:38:30.236
but the front ends
maybe shouldn't

00:38:30.236 --> 00:38:31.520
be able to talk to each other.

00:38:31.520 --> 00:38:32.936
And maybe your
front end shouldn't

00:38:32.936 --> 00:38:35.761
be able to go out to the public
internet and do other things.

00:38:35.761 --> 00:38:38.260
And we have some basic support
for network policy right now.

00:38:38.260 --> 00:38:39.790
It requires some
external plug-ins,

00:38:39.790 --> 00:38:41.790
and so we're going to
iterate on that over time.

00:38:43.842 --> 00:38:45.800
I also want to talk a
little bit about a couple

00:38:45.800 --> 00:38:46.610
of other features.

00:38:46.610 --> 00:38:50.030
So Jess talked about AppArmor
and seccomp and SELinux.

00:38:50.030 --> 00:38:53.840
We would like to have support
for more security contexts.

00:38:53.840 --> 00:38:57.200
So no new privileges is
a particular Linux flag,

00:38:57.200 --> 00:39:00.680
and it carries over between
fork and clone and execve.

00:39:00.680 --> 00:39:02.540
And what it does is it
stops new privileges

00:39:02.540 --> 00:39:04.310
from being added to a process.

00:39:04.310 --> 00:39:07.319
So that way, even if the initial
process can't do something,

00:39:07.319 --> 00:39:09.110
it can't then create
some separate process,

00:39:09.110 --> 00:39:11.390
give that other process
more permissions,

00:39:11.390 --> 00:39:15.350
and then compromise
your system that way.

00:39:15.350 --> 00:39:17.540
Another example of the kinds
of things we hear about

00:39:17.540 --> 00:39:19.370
is binary verification.

00:39:19.370 --> 00:39:23.020
So today, Kubernetes supports
an image policy web hook

00:39:23.020 --> 00:39:25.250
so that, for example, every
time a request comes in

00:39:25.250 --> 00:39:27.200
to create a pod to
run a container,

00:39:27.200 --> 00:39:29.810
it'll take that image and pass
it off to the image policy

00:39:29.810 --> 00:39:32.150
service, which can come
back with image allowed

00:39:32.150 --> 00:39:33.770
or image denied.

00:39:33.770 --> 00:39:36.240
We'd like to expand on that
basic support that's there

00:39:36.240 --> 00:39:38.660
and add audit longing for
the image policy check

00:39:38.660 --> 00:39:40.760
so that you can have
a full trail of what

00:39:40.760 --> 00:39:44.390
images were allowed, who
was trying to create them.

00:39:44.390 --> 00:39:46.790
And if you have
support for break glass

00:39:46.790 --> 00:39:49.220
to production emergency,
please just run it.

00:39:49.220 --> 00:39:51.470
Ignore that it's not signed.

00:39:51.470 --> 00:39:53.390
Make sure that that
gets logged as well.

00:39:53.390 --> 00:39:56.750
And so that what you can
do forensics afterwards

00:39:56.750 --> 00:39:59.870
to figure out what
happened on your cluster.

00:39:59.870 --> 00:40:03.140
We've also had some people
talk about binary verification

00:40:03.140 --> 00:40:04.610
of Kubernetes and its addons.

00:40:04.610 --> 00:40:07.280
So we're shipping all of
these official containers.

00:40:07.280 --> 00:40:09.470
Right now, they do not
have any attestation

00:40:09.470 --> 00:40:13.327
as to whether they're the right
container or system or not.

00:40:13.327 --> 00:40:15.410
And so one of the things
that might come over time

00:40:15.410 --> 00:40:17.450
is allowing the
Kubernetes itself

00:40:17.450 --> 00:40:19.490
to verify those
containers that it's

00:40:19.490 --> 00:40:22.537
running for its controllers,
the kube proxy, the DNS,

00:40:22.537 --> 00:40:24.620
the kubelet, and so on,
and make sure that it only

00:40:24.620 --> 00:40:27.207
runs fully verified system
components if that's something

00:40:27.207 --> 00:40:28.290
you'd like to have happen.

00:40:30.465 --> 00:40:32.090
So with that, I'm
going to switch gears

00:40:32.090 --> 00:40:35.030
to our last little section
here, Kubernetes 1.6.

00:40:35.030 --> 00:40:37.190
I'll talk about the next
releases after that.

00:40:37.190 --> 00:40:40.010
So we expect that to be
released in open source

00:40:40.010 --> 00:40:41.900
towards the end of March.

00:40:41.900 --> 00:40:43.520
Google Container
Engine should start

00:40:43.520 --> 00:40:47.330
picking it up and making it
available shortly thereafter.

00:40:47.330 --> 00:40:48.994
And then over time,
once we've verified

00:40:48.994 --> 00:40:50.660
that it's as stable
as we hope it to be,

00:40:50.660 --> 00:40:53.300
we would then do the
automatic upgrades

00:40:53.300 --> 00:40:56.740
to that version for
any existing clusters.

00:40:56.740 --> 00:40:58.240
So as far as
security model goes,

00:40:58.240 --> 00:41:00.730
we'll have a single
tenant with identities

00:41:00.730 --> 00:41:03.640
and the beginnings of
soft multi-tenancy.

00:41:03.640 --> 00:41:06.667
And pods should not have root
on the cluster by default.

00:41:06.667 --> 00:41:09.250
Some of the highlights is that
we're getting this RBAC system.

00:41:09.250 --> 00:41:10.666
That's been some
of the main focus

00:41:10.666 --> 00:41:12.249
over the last three months--

00:41:12.249 --> 00:41:14.290
getting that enabled by
default and giving people

00:41:14.290 --> 00:41:18.391
those richer capabilities
to control their system.

00:41:18.391 --> 00:41:20.140
We've also been adding
a lot of identities

00:41:20.140 --> 00:41:21.556
to the infrastructure
components--

00:41:21.556 --> 00:41:23.470
the nodes, the
controllers, and so on.

00:41:23.470 --> 00:41:25.690
And many of them have
more limited permissions

00:41:25.690 --> 00:41:31.180
than they do with 1.5 and
below, but not all of them.

00:41:31.180 --> 00:41:33.180
So Kubernetes is on a
three-month release cycle.

00:41:33.180 --> 00:41:36.360
So coming in June 2017,
near the end of that month,

00:41:36.360 --> 00:41:38.889
we expect Kubernetes
1.7 to ship.

00:41:38.889 --> 00:41:40.680
As far as the security
model for that goes,

00:41:40.680 --> 00:41:44.250
it should be a pretty
decent soft multi-tenancy.

00:41:44.250 --> 00:41:47.220
And in particular, if you are
able to escape a container,

00:41:47.220 --> 00:41:49.930
that escalation should be
limited to just the one

00:41:49.930 --> 00:41:51.639
node that it's running on.

00:41:51.639 --> 00:41:54.180
So some of the highlights from
a security perspective that we

00:41:54.180 --> 00:41:56.730
might expect to
see are finishing

00:41:56.730 --> 00:41:59.280
the limiting enforcement
of system components, such

00:41:59.280 --> 00:42:02.240
that each particular node can
only do what it wants to do,

00:42:02.240 --> 00:42:04.170
what it needs to do.

00:42:04.170 --> 00:42:06.420
We expect our audit
logging to get much more

00:42:06.420 --> 00:42:09.360
comprehensive to include many
more sources of what's going on

00:42:09.360 --> 00:42:12.180
in the cluster, and
possibly also have

00:42:12.180 --> 00:42:13.380
capabilities to ship those.

00:42:13.380 --> 00:42:16.230
Right now, it's written to
a file on the master itself.

00:42:16.230 --> 00:42:18.900
Ship those off to
network endpoints.

00:42:18.900 --> 00:42:22.554
And then we also hear a lot
about certificate rotation--

00:42:22.554 --> 00:42:24.720
being able to take those
certificates in your system

00:42:24.720 --> 00:42:26.940
and rotate them over time.

00:42:26.940 --> 00:42:29.610
There's more speculative bits,
things like improved secrets

00:42:29.610 --> 00:42:30.600
management.

00:42:30.600 --> 00:42:34.020
Right now, the secrets are
written to etcd as a database,

00:42:34.020 --> 00:42:36.270
and they're stored
in plain text.

00:42:36.270 --> 00:42:39.460
On Container Engine, the disk
itself is encrypted at REST.

00:42:39.460 --> 00:42:42.510
So we do have some amount of
encryption at REST for GKE.

00:42:42.510 --> 00:42:45.000
But in Kubernetes itself,
they're in plain text in etcd,

00:42:45.000 --> 00:42:47.250
so you really need to make
sure that people can't just

00:42:47.250 --> 00:42:50.800
read your etcd database.

00:42:50.800 --> 00:42:53.120
There's also talk about
runtime isolation improvements.

00:42:53.120 --> 00:42:55.164
So you saw how good
seccomp could be.

00:42:55.164 --> 00:42:56.580
We'd like to see
that maybe become

00:42:56.580 --> 00:43:00.746
the default in Kubernetes and
require people to say no, no, I

00:43:00.746 --> 00:43:03.120
have something that is going
to require extra special sys

00:43:03.120 --> 00:43:06.360
calls, and I'm going to trust
it to do that, give you this pod

00:43:06.360 --> 00:43:10.270
permissions to do
those extra things.

00:43:10.270 --> 00:43:12.580
Once we've done all that,
three months later, again,

00:43:12.580 --> 00:43:16.979
September 2017, that's when we
expect Kubernetes 1.8 to land.

00:43:16.979 --> 00:43:18.520
And at that point,
our security model

00:43:18.520 --> 00:43:20.980
is starting what will
probably be a long road

00:43:20.980 --> 00:43:23.980
to hard multi-tenancy.

00:43:23.980 --> 00:43:25.100
Unclear at this point.

00:43:25.100 --> 00:43:27.680
We're not looking forward
quite that far yet.

00:43:27.680 --> 00:43:30.910
We're still in the
1.6 shipping phase

00:43:30.910 --> 00:43:33.431
and starting to plan for 1.7.

00:43:33.431 --> 00:43:34.930
Some of the things
that we might see

00:43:34.930 --> 00:43:38.710
are VM-based runtimes via the
container runtime interface.

00:43:38.710 --> 00:43:41.890
That would provide an extra
layer of defense in depth.

00:43:41.890 --> 00:43:44.920
More binary verification,
potentially that better

00:43:44.920 --> 00:43:47.630
resource isolation between
containers that I mentioned.

00:43:47.630 --> 00:43:49.870
And then also
large organizations

00:43:49.870 --> 00:43:52.390
might like to be able
to specify policy.

00:43:52.390 --> 00:43:55.300
Everyone in my org should run
with read only file systems.

00:43:55.300 --> 00:43:57.040
You can't create a
pod in my service

00:43:57.040 --> 00:44:00.490
that has that flag disabled--

00:44:00.490 --> 00:44:04.930
unless, of course, you're
in the special whitelist.

00:44:04.930 --> 00:44:06.370
And so to give
you a brief recap,

00:44:06.370 --> 00:44:07.667
we talked about four things--

00:44:07.667 --> 00:44:10.000
security boundaries, we talked
about identity and access

00:44:10.000 --> 00:44:13.510
management, the runtime
security, and Jess's demo,

00:44:13.510 --> 00:44:15.190
and then a little
bit of a roadmap

00:44:15.190 --> 00:44:17.307
for Kubernetes security.

00:44:17.307 --> 00:44:19.390
So with that, I'd like to
invite people to come up

00:44:19.390 --> 00:44:21.970
to the two mics for questions.

00:44:21.970 --> 00:44:24.210
If anyone wants to chat
privately afterwards--

00:44:24.210 --> 00:44:25.740
as privately as you can her--

00:44:25.740 --> 00:44:27.490
we'll go outside if
we run over time,

00:44:27.490 --> 00:44:29.080
and you can meet
us in the hallway.

00:44:29.080 --> 00:44:32.130
[APPLAUSE]

